758	/* :not(.nx-dark-island .glass) — the promo carousel's sheet is a `.glass`
759	   sitting over its OWN photographic artwork and scrim, tuned with literal
760	   pale-blue/white text throughout (never page tokens), same as a hero. The
761	   unscoped version of this rule turned it into a bright white pane with
762	   still-white text on top of it — invisible, not readable. */
763	html[data-theme="light"] .glass:not(.nx-dark-island .glass),
764	html[data-theme="light"] .glass-sm:not(.nx-dark-island .glass-sm) {
765	  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72);
766	  border-color: var(--color-rim);
767	  /* `.glass`'s box-shadow (the "liquid glass" block further down this file)
768	     is a set of literal insets tuned for a dark material: a near-opaque
769	     white line lighting the top edge, a broad white glow bleeding 18-32px
770	     into the body, and a 50%-black shadow weighting the bottom third. None
771	     of those are page tokens, so flipping to a white background above did
772	     nothing to them — the result was white-on-white glare at the top and a
773	     dirty grey smear at the bottom of every `.glass` panel in light mode
774	     (the mail reply composer's popout, reported as "washed out, hazy,
775	     frosted glass gone wrong on a light background"). Swapped for the same
776	     restrained lift `.card` gets in light mode: a hairline shadow plus a
777	     soft ink-tinted drop, no directional "light source" baked in. */
778	  box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.06), 0 20px 44px -22px rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.28);
779	}
780	/* `.glass`'s ::before (a lens-edge hairline) and ::after (a top-third gloss)
781	   are both literal white-on-white gradients whose entire job is to catch
782	   light on a dark material — there is no light-mode equivalent to swap in
783	   short of inventing a new effect (which the rest of this file deliberately
784	   avoids), so they are switched off rather than left painting extra white
785	   haze over an already-white panel. `.glass-sm` never defined these two, so
